<ArticleLayout slug="cost-to-localize-a-campaign-for-china" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your campaign" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Search this and you get translation platforms quoting cents per word. That answer is not wrong so much as aimed at a different problem. The expensive part of a China adaptation is not the words. It is that the picture was composed for a frame China does not use, carrying text that is a legal object here, featuring a person whose release does not cover this territory.</p>

  <h2>How much does it cost to adapt a campaign for China?</h2>

  <p>There is no single number, and anyone printing one is guessing. What can be done honestly is to itemize the stack, put a sourced band on the lines that have one, and mark the lines that have no published benchmark at all as exactly that.</p>

  <p>Seven lines carry the cost: layout adaptation, re-shoot or recasting, platform variants, copy transcreation, compliance review, AI labeling, and upload QA. A translation quote covers part of one of them.</p>

  <p>One framing point before the detail. This work comes out of the production budget, which is the line that has been shrinking.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production averaged 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025. Survey of senior marketing procurement respondents at 50 global brands across more than ten sectors.</p>

  <h2>Is China localization just translation?</h2>

  <p>No, and the reason is structural rather than cultural. Three things about a Western master stop being true the moment it enters a Chinese surface, and none of them is fixed by changing the language.</p>

  <p>The frame is different. The on-image text becomes a regulated object. And the person in the picture is licensed for somewhere else.</p>

  <p>Translation vendors are not hiding this. They are answering the question they were asked, and they price it clearly.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in the translation category put professional English to Simplified Chinese at roughly 0.08 to 0.18 USD per source word, and price transcreation per hour or per project instead, because source word count does not track the creative work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages collected across the category, September 2026, no company named. Rate cards only, no survey and no method published behind the band.</p>

  <p>Read the second half of that. The category itself stops pricing per word as soon as the work becomes creative, which is the category conceding the point this page is making.</p>

  <h2>Why can a Western master not be re-cropped for China?</h2>

  <p>Four reasons, strongest first. The first is arithmetic you can check yourself.</p>

  <p><strong>The typography has nowhere to live.</strong> Take a 16:9 master and cut it to a tall frame at full height. A 3:4 frame keeps about 42 percent of the original width. A 4:5 frame keeps about 45 percent. A 9:16 frame keeps about 32 percent. A 2.39:1 cinema master cut to 9:16 keeps about 24 percent.</p>

  <p>Those are ratios divided by ratios, not measurements: 0.75, 0.8 and 0.5625 divided by 1.778, and 0.5625 divided by 2.39. Whatever the exact frame a given surface wants, a wide master surrenders half to three quarters of its width to reach a tall one. The subject sat on a third that no longer exists, the negative space that carried the headline is gone, and the product has moved relative to the edge. That is a rebuild, not a crop.</p>

  <p><strong>The on-image text is a legal object here.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Advertising Law bans "national level," "highest grade," "best" and any term of the same or similar meaning in advertising, and sets a fine of 200,000 to 1,000,000 RMB on the advertiser for a breach, with the same band on the agency and the publisher.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 9(3) and 57, 2021 amendment. Statutory text, no sample.</p>

  <p>Superlatives are the ordinary grammar of Western advertising headlines, and a good share of them are baked into artwork rather than sitting in a caption field. Taking them off means re-typesetting and re-exporting every size, which is a production job, not a copy edit. Note also that the penalty band lands on the agency and the publisher too, not only the advertiser.</p>

  <p>The rule is narrower than its reputation, and it is worth knowing where the edges are.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's market regulator published Enforcement Guidelines on Absolute Terms in Advertising as Announcement 2023 No. 6, adopted 24 February 2023 and published 20 March 2023, setting out where absolute wording is not caught and where a first, promptly corrected breach draws no penalty.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: State Administration for Market Regulation announcement, 20 March 2023. The regulator's own published guideline, twelve articles.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Beijing's market regulator issued an implementing work guideline on 20 March 2024 stating that absolute wording is caught only where it points at the goods being sold and has the effect of misleading consumers or disparaging other traders.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Beijing Municipal Market Supervision Administration work guideline and official Q&amp;A, 20 March 2024. The regulator's own text.</p>

  <p>So a superlative about the brand's ambition reads differently from a superlative about the product's rank. That distinction is a review decision on every line of on-image copy, and it is work whether the answer is yes or no.</p>

  <p><strong>The ad has to declare itself.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, in force 1 May 2023, an advertisement must be identifiable as one, and any post that promotes goods through knowledge sharing, experience sharing or consumer review formats while carrying a purchase link must be conspicuously marked "advertisement."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, Article 9, State Administration for Market Regulation Order No. 72, issued 25 February 2023. Statutory text.</p>

  <p>That mark is a layout element the master never had. It changes the safe area on every frame it appears in, which means it has to be designed rather than stamped on at upload.</p>

  <p><strong>The likeness rights do not travel.</strong></p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's Civil Code, a likeness may not be made, used or published without the portrayed person's consent; where a licence clause is disputed it is construed in favor of the person portrayed; and where the licence term is unagreed or unclear either party may end it on reasonable notice.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Civil Code of the People's Republic of China, Articles 1019, 1021 and 1022, in force 1 January 2021. Statutory text.</p>

  <p>Read the middle clause. Ambiguity is resolved against the party using the image. A release written for a European campaign that does not name Chinese platforms, Chinese territory or a defined term is not a document you want to rely on, and the third clause means an unclear term can be ended on notice after the campaign is live.</p>

  <p>The choice is re-clearance or recasting, and re-clearance is often the slower of the two. Worth saying plainly: this is a rights and logistics argument, not a claim about what audiences prefer. No source supports the idea that locally cast talent performs better, so this page does not make it.</p>

  <h2>How many assets does a China adaptation produce?</h2>

  <p>More than the master count, because each surface wants a structurally different object rather than a resized one.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Surface</th>
          <th scope="col">What it structurally demands</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Marketplace product pages</th>
          <td>A square catalog frame plus a long scrolling detail page, authored separately</td>
          <td>The detail page is a document, not a resized ad</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Short video</th>
          <td>A tall frame with a moving safe area, plus an ad mark where a purchase path exists</td>
          <td>Frame arithmetic and the Internet Advertising Measures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Social discovery</th>
          <td>A cover plus a card sequence, so one hero does not fill the unit</td>
          <td>Format-inherent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Messaging and owned</th>
          <td>An article layout with its own cover crop, distinct from every ad frame</td>
          <td>Format-inherent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Broadcast social</th>
          <td>A feed card crop, plus the ad mark where a purchase path is attached</td>
          <td>Internet Advertising Measures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Livestream</th>
          <td>Scripted claims and on-screen supers become compliance objects</td>
          <td>Advertiser and endorser duties attach by role</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The livestream row is the one that surprises budget-holders.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same Measures put advertiser duties on a seller who promotes by livestream, and agency, publisher or endorser duties on the room operator and the on-camera talent depending on their role.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, Article 19, in force 1 May 2023. Statutory text.</p>

  <p>Duties attach by what someone actually does in the room. So the script, the supers and the product claims need the same review the artwork got, and that review is a line item.</p>

  <h2>The labeling line nobody budgeted for</h2>

  <p>If any part of the adaptation is generated, a second regime applies, and it lands on the file itself.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Measures for Labeling AI-Generated Synthetic Content, issued 14 March 2025 by four central bodies and effective 1 September 2025, require a user-perceivable label on generated images and video and a second, machine label written into the file metadata carrying the synthetic attribute, the provider identifier and a content number, and require that exported and downloaded copies still carry the visible label.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China announcement, 14 March 2025. The regulator's own published instrument, effective 1 September 2025.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A mandatory national standard on labeling methods for AI-generated synthetic content took effect alongside those Measures on 1 September 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national mandatory standard GB 45438-2025, in force 1 September 2025. Standards body approval, confirmed in state wire coverage the same day.</p>

  <p>Two labels, one visible and one in the metadata, and the visible one has to survive export and download. Most adaptation pipelines flatten and re-export files repeatedly, which is precisely where a visible label gets lost and a metadata label gets stripped. Budget the QA pass, because the requirement is about what arrives at the other end.</p>

  <p>None of this is theoretical enforcement.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's market regulator handled 46,900 illegal advertising cases in 2024 with 349 million RMB in fines and forfeitures, of which more than 30,000 cases and 187 million RMB were internet advertising.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: State Administration for Market Regulation full-year enforcement totals, released 14 March 2025. National case counts published by the regulator at a press briefing.</p>

  <p>More than 30,000 internet advertising cases in one year. Whatever the odds on any single asset, this is an actively policed area rather than a dormant one.</p>

