  <p>Six of those nine lines have no published benchmark anywhere. That is not this page being coy. It is the actual state of the category, and it is why a translation quote is the only number most buyers ever see: it is the only line with a public price.</p>

  <h2>Two worked stacks</h2>

  <p>Built from published cards only, each line carrying its own attribution. They deliberately do not total, because the lines come from different sources with different units and a headline sum would imply a precision none of them has.</p>

  <p><strong>A catalog-led adaptation.</strong> A brand bringing an existing product range onto Chinese marketplace pages, no film, no talent.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese commercial photography studio's published 2026 rate card lists flat-lay shots at 80 to 150 RMB an image, in-studio model shots at 200 to 300 RMB, retouching at 50 to 200 RMB an image and short video at 3,000 to 15,000 RMB a piece, on a five-style minimum.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published Chinese ecommerce photography rate card, dated 20 July 2026, described by its publisher as its own executing rates for the Guangzhou and Pearl River Delta region. One studio's book, no survey behind it.</p>

  <p>That is one studio's book in one region, published on one date, and it is quoted here as exactly that rather than as a market average. Against it sits the layout line, which is where the published evidence falls apart.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published Chinese rate cards for ecommerce detail-page design disagree by roughly an order of magnitude, from 200 to 300 RMB at the low end of a 2023 page to 1,500 to 5,000 RMB a set on a 2026 page. None of them states what a set contains, and none states a sample or a method.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published Chinese design rate cards collected September 2026, dated between June 2023 and April 2026, no company named. Published prices only, conflict stated rather than averaged.</p>

  <p>An order of magnitude, and nobody defines the unit. That disagreement is not a research failure to apologize for. It is precisely what a buyer walks into when they ask three suppliers for a detail-page quote and get three numbers that cannot be compared, because none of them says what a set contains.</p>

  <p>So the catalog stack reads: capture per image against a dated regional card, retouch per image against the same card, layout against a range that must be pinned to a defined set before it means anything, plus compliance review and upload QA with no benchmark at all.</p>

  <p><strong>A film-led adaptation.</strong> A brand with an existing hero film and a Western cast.</p>

  <p>Here the first question is not a price, it is the release. If the talent agreement does not name Chinese territory, Chinese platforms and a defined term, the Civil Code position above makes reliance on it a poor bet, and the stack forks: re-clearance, which is a legal cost and a calendar risk, or a recast and re-shoot, which is a production cost with a known shape. The film also needs the tall-frame rebuild described earlier, and short video sits at 3,000 to 15,000 RMB a piece on that same published card.</p>

  <p>Both stacks then carry the labeling line if anything in them is generated, and both carry a QA pass that verifies the visible label survived export.</p>

  <h2>How to scope this before anyone quotes</h2>

  <p>Four things to settle, because they are what makes the quotes comparable.</p>

  <p><strong>Define the set.</strong> Decide what one delivered unit is, per surface, before you ask for a price. The order-of-magnitude spread above exists mostly because nobody defines it.</p>

  <p><strong>Audit the releases first.</strong> Not after the creative is chosen. Whether the talent travels decides whether this is an adaptation or a re-shoot, and that is the largest fork in the whole stack.</p>

  <p><strong>Read your own headlines against Article 9(3).</strong> Mark every superlative and decide, line by line, whether it points at the goods. Do it before layout, because the answer changes the artwork.</p>

  <p><strong>Count the surfaces, not the assets.</strong> Each surface wants a structurally different object. Counting assets from the master will undercount, every time.</p>

