  <p>Reviewed September 2026. Every figure above is dated, and the compliance instruments are cited by article rather than through commentary. This describes production practice and is not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about China localization</h2>

  <h3>How much does it cost to adapt a campaign for China?</h3>

  <p>There is no single published figure, and six of the nine cost lines have no public benchmark at all. What is published: translation at roughly 0.08 to 0.18 USD per source word, capture from 80 to 300 RMB an image on one dated regional card, and detail-page layout spanning 200 to 5,000 RMB depending on which card you read.</p>

  <h3>Is China localization just translation?</h3>

  <p>No. Translation covers the words, which is roughly one line of a seven-line stack. The frame changes, the on-image text becomes a regulated object under the Advertising Law, an ad mark has to be designed into the layout, and Western talent releases rarely cover Chinese territory or platforms.</p>

  <h3>Why can a Western master not be re-cropped for China?</h3>

  <p>Cutting a 16:9 master to a tall frame keeps about 32 to 45 percent of its width, which is ratio arithmetic rather than a measurement. The headline's negative space and the subject's placement both go. Add re-typesetting to remove superlatives, a required ad mark, and talent rights that do not travel.</p>

  <h3>What does a translation quote leave out?</h3>

  <p>Re-typesetting every size, the layout rebuild for tall frames, re-shoot or recasting, talent re-clearance, compliance review of on-image claims, AI label application and survival QA, and upload QA per surface. Only the words and sometimes transcreation are inside a translation quote.</p>

  <h3>How many assets does a China adaptation produce?</h3>

  <p>More than the master count, because each surface demands a structurally different object rather than a resize: a square catalog frame plus a separate long detail page, a tall video frame with a moving safe area, a cover plus card sequence, an article layout with its own cover crop. Count surfaces first, then assets.</p>
